WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.920
. So this next story, this this segment I'm
going to do right now, this is the prototypical

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.730
segment that I think is some of the best stuff
that we do.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.200
And it does terribly in terms of audience.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.149
But it is so interesting and it is so worth
doing and so informative.

00:00:19.149 --> 00:00:20.320
Let's dig right into it.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.130
I told you previously that Joe Biden will
be selecting Janet Yellen as his treasury

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.370
secretary.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.309
And we talked about Janet Yellen s politics.

00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:33.870
And I pointed out there are things that should
make progressives reasonably happy about the

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.590
selection of Janet Yellen.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:39.930
There are things that might make progressives
not so happy about Janet Yellen.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.640
But the big picture about Janet Yellen as
treasury secretary is that anything that Joe

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.480
Biden is able to get done with the House and
Senate that he ends up having, Janet Yellen

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:54.100
is not likely to stand in the way at all,
meaning that she will not be the impediment

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.860
to Joe Biden getting his campaign platform
done.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.579
She's a relatively good selection.

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:00.640
She's the first woman in that position.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.290
And she's a true economic.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:06.830
She's she's an academic.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.510
She's an academic from the field of economics.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:14.540
She is also beyond that, a really clever economist.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:21.010
And I was doing some research on her and came
across an article that she published along

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:27.010
with George Akerlof in the quarterly Journal
of Economics back in May of 1990.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.720
This is 30 years ago now.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:35.729
OK, and she published a paper about the so-called
fair wage effort hypothesis.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:42.100
This is not just economics, it's social psychology
and it's sociology and it's brilliant and

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.409
fascinating.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.159
And it just reinforces that Joe Biden is picking
serious people.

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:52.939
Donald Trump's treasury secretary is Stephen
Manoogian, who was a hedge fund guy who lived

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:57.320
in a hotel and drove a Porsche while he was
getting his undergraduate degree and then

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.780
went and worked, you know, in big finance,
hedge funds, et cetera.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.909
Janet Yellen is a Ph.D. in economics and has
published scholarly articles and developed

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:12.689
original ideas of her own and the idea that
she has developed in this fair wage effort

00:02:12.689 --> 00:02:13.689
hypothesis.

00:02:13.689 --> 00:02:18.689
This is, again, 30 years old, but it's talking
about the rigor, academic rigor that Janet

00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:19.689
Yellen is capable of.

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:21.730
The idea is the following.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:28.209
If your wage that you are paid falls short
of your perception of what would be a fair

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:33.519
wage for that job, you start to not work as
hard and you're less productive.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:40.579
The idea of hiring someone else wouldn't work
unless you can find someone who really doesn't

00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:47.480
understand how their wage relates to what
would be a fair wage for the job you've hired

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.790
them for.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:51.290
And we've actually observed this phenomenon.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:57.069
You will you often will end up seeing people
with different skill levels doing the same

00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:02.790
job and getting paid the same amount and they
end up with almost identical productivity

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:03.930
would be strange intuitively.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:04.930
Right?

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.749
I mean, you've got two people doing the same
work.

00:03:07.749 --> 00:03:12.239
One is significantly more skilled in ways
that are relevant to that work.

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:17.609
But because of this pressure that Janet Yellen
describes that exists where you are comparing

00:03:17.609 --> 00:03:24.249
your wage to the fair wage, even if you are
more skilled and you would be able to be significantly

00:03:24.249 --> 00:03:29.700
more productive, you will produce down to
the level you believe is appropriate for the

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.099
wage you are being paid.

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:33.040
Very, very interesting.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.760
There are lots of studies in terms of people
having a perception of the fairness of the

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.319
wage they're being paid.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:45.129
One such study is Lawler and O'Gara, which
you can Google if you're interested in this.

00:03:45.129 --> 00:03:50.549
And they did some really simple but well controlled
experiments where they would pay a set amount

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:51.909
per task.

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:57.390
And when they paid less for the same task,
given equally qualified and skilled individuals,

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.540
when you paid less, productivity went down.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.370
Now, if you're interested in this, we're linking
actually to Janet Yellen article in the description

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.819
for the YouTube video on this story.

00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:14.169
But it's really quite elegant because it directly
refutes a lot of really bad right wing talking

00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:17.000
points about the minimum wage and wages in
general.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.370
And I'll give you some examples of this.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:25.200
There's this talking point from the right
where it's sort of like a reductio ad absurdum

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.180
where they say, listen, if we want people
to have more money and paying them more is

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.510
better, why stop at a 15 dollar minimum wage?

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.910
Why not make it one hundred and fifty dollars
an hour or a thousand dollars an hour?

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.420
Just pay people, make everybody multimillionaires.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:45.030
Now, intuitively, intuitively, we know that
that's idiotic.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.800
And one of the reasons it doesn't make sense
is that paying people one hundred and fifty

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.350
dollars or a thousand dollars instead of just
a reasonable minimum wage, it would not be

00:04:54.350 --> 00:05:00.120
an equilibrium wage that makes any sense in
terms of the cost of living nor relative to

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.120
other professions.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.940
It doesn't make any sense.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:09.541
But what Janet Yellen hypothesis adds here
is that paying somebody saying, well, let's

00:05:09.541 --> 00:05:15.520
set the minimum wage to 150 or a thousand,
it wouldn't be in line with a fair wage.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:22.520
If you survey people about what would be a
fair wage for traditional minimum wage work,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.900
it's not going to be one hundred and fifty
dollars or a thousand dollars an hour.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.770
It'll be much closer to the fifteen dollars
an hour.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.090
That's widely now being discussed.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.910
I'll give you another right wing talking point
that is implicitly debunked by Janet Yellen

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.240
hypothesis.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:43.070
If you increase the minimum wage, you force
businesses to lay off employees and it increases

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:44.070
unemployment.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.320
Well, hold on a second.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:52.181
If Janet Yellen is right and the literature
suggests she is, if you pay people less, you're

00:05:52.181 --> 00:05:57.180
making them less productive, which means you
either need to pay them to work longer hours

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.400
or you need to hire more of them to get to
the same level of productivity that costs

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.990
you more as the employer as well.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:05.990
The other one, you.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:11.580
Here, which is similar, is you raise the minimum
wage, you raise the price of consumer goods,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:17.600
well, the same analysis applies if you lower
the wage and you make employees less productive

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.180
as a result, you still need more employees
or you need employees working more hours to

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:28.570
produce your stuff, you'd theoretically still
need to raise the price of the goods that

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:29.740
you are selling.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:34.190
So it's quite elegant, very interesting stuff.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.900
And again, read it.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:37.710
It's only about eight, 10 pages.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:42.560
If you don't include all of the charts and
data at the end, you could read it in 10,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:43.620
15 minutes.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.140
But you will very quickly realize that we
are going in the direction of having actual

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.920
expert adults in positions of power upcoming.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.260
And we will have our political disagreements
with them.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:59.270
But we should all be welcoming this after
the fiasco charade that the Trump administration

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:00.270
has been.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.270
Check out the article.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.270
Let me know your thoughts.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:04.210
I'm on Twitter at David Pakman.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:04.822
We'll take a quick break and be back after
this.

